# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js project following the official Vercel documentation.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package (version ^2.0.1)
- Updated `package.json` to include the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the resolved dependencies

### 2. Analytics Integration
Modified `web/app/layout.tsx`:
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added `<Analytics />` component at the end of the `<body>` tag in the RootLayout component

This follows the official Next.js App Router integration pattern from the Vercel Analytics quickstart guide.

## Implementation Details

The Analytics component was placed just before the closing `</body>` tag, which is the recommended location according to Vercel's documentation. This ensures that:
- The analytics script loads without blocking the main content
- All page views and interactions are properly tracked
- The component integrates seamlessly with Next.js's server-side rendering

## Framework Detected
- **Framework**: Next.js 16.2.11 (App Router)
- **Package Manager**: npm (detected by presence of package-lock.json)
- **React Version**: 19.2.4

## Verification Steps Completed

1. ✅ TypeScript type checking: Passed with no errors
2. ✅ ESLint linting: Passed (1 pre-existing warning unrelated to changes)
3. ✅ Next.js production build: Completed successfully
4. ✅ All pages compiled and optimized correctly

## Next Steps

After deployment to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in your Vercel project dashboard (Analytics section)
2. Deploy the application
3. Analytics data will start appearing in the Vercel dashboard

## Files Modified
